 ### [Voxel-based 3D Fire Simulation](3411-voxel-based-3d-fire-simulation.md)
-Simulates fire dynamics by storing velocity, smoke density, temperature, and fuel level attributes in a discrete 3D voxel grid. It solves the Navier-Stokes equations to propagate fluid motion, augmented by specific buoyancy and vorticity forces to model the rising, curling behavior of fire. It is distinguished from particle-based methods by its structured grid storage, which allows for efficient divergence and advection calculations using 3D textures. Choose this when visual fidelity of fire dynamics (ignition, burning, extinction) is required.
+Simulates fire dynamics by storing velocity, smoke density, temperature, and fuel level attributes in a discrete 3D voxel grid. It solves the Navier-Stokes equations to propagate fluid motion, augmented by specific buoyancy and vorticity forces to model the rising, curling behavior of fire. It is distinguished from particle-based methods by its structured grid storage, which allows for efficient divergence and advection calculations using 3D textures. Choose this when visual fidelity of fire dynamics (ignition, burning, extinction) is needed [G].
 
 ### [Smoke Advection](4639-smoke-advection.md)
-Transports scalar quantities, such as density and temperature, through a fluid velocity field to visualize smoke effects in real-time simulations. It distinguishes itself from pure velocity advection by introducing source terms (e.g., Gaussian splats) and coupling scalar fields (like temperature) back into the dynamics via buoyant forces, rather than treating them as passive tracers alone. Choose this when smoke appearance and thermal effects are critical.
