 ### Buoyant Force for Smoke
-Adds a buoyant force to the velocity field based on temperature differences to make hot smoke rise and cool smoke fall [S2, unofficial]. The buoyant force is calculated as $\mathbf{f}_{buoyancy} = \frac{Pmg}{R} \left( \frac{1}{T_0} - \frac{1}{T} \right) \mathbf{z}$, where $P$ is pressure, $m$ is molar mass, $g$ is gravity, $R$ is the universal gas constant, $T_0$ is ambient temperature, $T$ is advected temperature, and $\mathbf{z}$ is the normalized upward vector [S2, unofficial]. It differs from neutral buoyancy simulations by introducing temperature-dependent vertical acceleration. Choose this when realistic smoke rise and cooling effects are required.
